    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Honda Racing Releases Its First-Ever In-House Made Parts for the Civic Type R and Integra Type S\nHeybike V1 Pro Lands with Everything You Want from an e-Bike for Just Under $2,000\n16 photos\nPhoto: Heybike\nLife is not easy these days for people in the market for electric two-wheelers, as they have so many options to choose from that, in some cases at least, could cause headaches. If the main criteria when choosing one bike over the other has to do with as many features as possible offered in a single, cheap package, then the Heybike V1 Pro might be the thing for you.\nHeybike was founded about five years ago, and, even if it's based in Texas, its roots are Chinese. The crew truly stepped into the spotlight earlier this year, when it presented the V1 model .\nThe company says the model was so successful (no numbers are provided to back up that claim, though) that it now requires a more capable variant. Called the V1 Pro, it takes the innate capabilities of the original model and stretches them even further.\nWhat does that mean? Well, whereas the basic setup of the Pro remains the same as the standard version, it lands on the market with double the peak power, almost 44 percent more battery capacity (which translates to 20 percent more range), 11 percent more top speed, and 50 percent higher climbing angle – because yes, this thing was not born to be used only on asphalt and level roads.\nOfficially, the Heybike V1 Pro is a dirt bike , and, as such, it packs the appropriate staggered wheels: 17 inches at the front and 14 inches at the rear, both of them shod in oversized Big Foot tires. The rear wheel gets over 30 percent more power compared to the regular V1 from the 8,000 W peak-power electric motor, and 250 Nm of torque.\nEnough to give the e-bike a top speed of up to 50 mph (80 kph). That can be maintained for as many as 60 miles (96 km), which is the maximum range the 1,944 Wh battery pack can allow on a single charge. Speaking of that, it takes up to six hours for the pack to get back to capacity when depleted.\nWhat makes this thing truly a dirt bike is the suspension system, which comprises a 150 mm-travel front fork and a 40 mm rear hydraulic shock. Coming it with 285 mm of ground clearance (almost 15 percent more than on the V1), the Pro can tackle inclines of up to 45 degrees.\nHeybike fitted the two-wheeler with a wheelie boost assist feature, because you never know when the rider gets the urge to show off. It kicks in to help the human in charge lift the front wheel to a fixed (and undisclosed) angle.\nHydraulic disc brakes (with regenerative braking) are on deck to handle stopping, and there is even a magnetic emergency kill switch on deck that cuts power should the rider fall off the bike. At the same time, a throttle-reset safety mechanism stops power delivery to the wheels.\nHeybike offers the Pro with a dedicated app that allows riders, among other things, to select one of three modes (Eco, Linear, and Sport), while an optional Bluetooth speaker is offered as an option, with the added perks of multiple simulated motorcycle engine sounds, as the company says.\nThe Heybike V1 Pro is already available to pre-order in the United States, where, for now at least, it goes for $1,999 for a limited period of time. If you happen to miss this window (it's unclear how long it will be open), the price jumps to $2,199.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHeyBike heybike v1 heybike v1 pro dirt bike E-Bike\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Gran Turismo 7 Will Let You Drive a 1954 Maserati Spyder for Free for Two Weeks",
